{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000112",
  "title": "Maze Generator",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": "// \u2500\u2500 Wall Bitmask Constants \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n// Each cell stores a bitmask of REMOVED walls (carved passages).\n\npub const N: u8 = 1;\npub const E: u8 = 2;\npub const S: u8 = 4;\npub const W: u8 = 8;\n\npub const MAZE_W: usize = 25;\npub const MAZE_H: usize = 25;\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Deterministic PRNG (xorshift64) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\npub struct Rng(pub u64);\n\nimpl Rng {\n    pub fn next_u64(&mut self) -> u64 {\n        self.0 ^= self.0 << 13;\n        self.0 ^= self.0 >> 7;\n        self.0 ^= self.0 << 17;\n        self.0\n    }\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Maze Generation (Recursive Backtracker) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\npub fn generate_maze(seed: u64) -> Vec<u8> {\n    let w = MAZE_W;\n    let h = MAZE_H;\n    let mut rng = Rng(seed);\n\n    // Grid where each cell stores a bitmask of carved passages\n    let mut grid = vec![0u8; w * h];\n    let mut visited = vec![false; w * h];\n    let mut stack: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n\n    // Start from top-left corner\n    visited[0] = true;\n    stack.push(0);\n\n    while let Some(&current) = stack.last() {\n        let cx = current % w;\n        let cy = current / w;\n\n        // Collect unvisited neighbors: (neighbor_idx, wall_from_current, wall_from_neighbor)\n        let mut neighbors: Vec<(usize, u8, u8)> = Vec::new();\n        if cy > 0 && !visited[current - w] {\n            neighbors.push((current - w, N, S));\n        }\n        if cx < w - 1 && !visited[current + 1] {\n            neighbors.push((current + 1, E, W));\n        }\n        if cy < h - 1 && !visited[current + w] {\n            neighbors.push((current + w, S, N));\n        }\n        if cx > 0 && !visited[current - 1] {\n            neighbors.push((current - 1, W, E));\n        }\n\n        if neighbors.is_empty() {\n            // Dead end \u2014 backtrack\n            stack.pop();\n        } else {\n            // Pick a random unvisited neighbor\n            let idx = (rng.next_u64() as usize) % neighbors.len();\n            let (next, wall_from, wall_to) = neighbors[idx];\n\n            // Carve passage between current and next\n            grid[current] |= wall_from;\n            grid[next] |= wall_to;\n\n            visited[next] = true;\n            stack.push(next);\n        }\n    }\n\n    grid\n}\n",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "> *AI authored to showcase ironpad capabilities.*\n\n# Maze Generator\n\n**Maze generation** algorithms create perfect mazes \u2014 mazes where every cell is reachable from every other cell, with exactly one path between any two points.\n\n## Recursive Backtracker Algorithm\n\nThe **recursive backtracker** (a depth-first search variant) is one of the most popular maze generators:\n\n1. Start at a cell, mark it **visited**\n2. While the stack is not empty:\n   - Look at the **current cell's unvisited neighbors**\n   - If there are unvisited neighbors: pick one at **random**, carve a passage to it, push it onto the stack\n   - If there are none: **backtrack** (pop the stack)\n\nThis produces mazes with long, winding corridors and relatively few dead ends \u2014 characteristic of DFS-based generators.\n\n## Representation\n\nEach cell stores a **bitmask** of carved walls: `N=1, E=2, S=4, W=8`. If a bit is set, the wall in that direction has been removed (there's a passage).",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Generate Maze",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Generate a 25x25 maze using the recursive backtracker algorithm.\nlet grid = shared::generate_maze(12345);\n\nCellOutput::text(format!(\n    \"Generated {}x{} maze ({} cells, {} bytes of wall data)\",\n    shared::MAZE_W,\n    shared::MAZE_H,\n    grid.len(),\n    grid.len(),\n))\n",
      "cargo_toml": null,
      "collapsed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Render Maze",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use ironpad_cell::prelude::*;\n\n// Generate and render the maze as a Canvas with dark theme.\nlet grid = shared::generate_maze(12345);\nlet (w, h) = (shared::MAZE_W, shared::MAZE_H);\n\nlet grid_w = 2 * w + 1;\nlet grid_h = 2 * h + 1;\n\n// Build logical pixel grid: false = wall, true = passage\nlet mut pixels = vec![false; grid_w * grid_h];\n\nfor cy in 0..h {\n    for cx in 0..w {\n        let walls = grid[cy * w + cx];\n        let px = 2 * cx + 1;\n        let py = 2 * cy + 1;\n\n        pixels[py * grid_w + px] = true; // cell interior\n\n        if walls & shared::N != 0 { pixels[(py - 1) * grid_w + px] = true; }\n        if walls & shared::E != 0 { pixels[py * grid_w + (px + 1)] = true; }\n        if walls & shared::S != 0 { pixels[(py + 1) * grid_w + px] = true; }\n        if walls & shared::W != 0 { pixels[py * grid_w + (px - 1)] = true; }\n    }\n}\n\nconst SCALE: u32 = 16;\nlet entrance = (1usize, 1usize);\nlet exit = (2 * (w - 1) + 1, 2 * (h - 1) + 1);\n\nlet canvas = Canvas::from_fn(grid_w as u32 * SCALE, grid_h as u32 * SCALE, |x, y| {\n    let lx = (x / SCALE) as usize;\n    let ly = (y / SCALE) as usize;\n\n    if (lx, ly) == entrance { return (80, 220, 120); }\n    if (lx, ly) == exit { return (220, 80, 80); }\n\n    if pixels[ly * grid_w + lx] {\n        (200, 200, 220)\n    } else {\n        (40, 40, 60)\n    }\n});\n\nCellOutput::from(canvas)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null,
      "collapsed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Solve & Render",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use ironpad_cell::prelude::*;\n\n// Generate the same maze, solve it with BFS, and render the solution path.\nlet grid = shared::generate_maze(12345);\nlet (w, h) = (shared::MAZE_W, shared::MAZE_H);\n\n// BFS from entrance (0,0) to exit (w-1, h-1)\nlet start = 0_usize;\nlet goal = w * h - 1;\n\nlet mut parent = vec![usize::MAX; w * h];\nlet mut visited = vec![false; w * h];\nlet mut queue = std::collections::VecDeque::new();\n\nvisited[start] = true;\nqueue.push_back(start);\n\nwhile let Some(current) = queue.pop_front() {\n    if current == goal {\n        break;\n    }\n\n    let cx = current % w;\n    let cy = current / w;\n    let walls = grid[current];\n\n    let neighbors = [\n        (walls & shared::N != 0 && cy > 0, current.wrapping_sub(w)),\n        (walls & shared::E != 0 && cx < w - 1, current + 1),\n        (walls & shared::S != 0 && cy < h - 1, current + w),\n        (walls & shared::W != 0 && cx > 0, current.wrapping_sub(1)),\n    ];\n\n    for (passable, next) in neighbors {\n        if passable && !visited[next] {\n            visited[next] = true;\n            parent[next] = current;\n            queue.push_back(next);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Reconstruct the solution path\nlet mut path = Vec::new();\nlet mut cur = goal;\nwhile cur != usize::MAX {\n    path.push(cur);\n    cur = parent[cur];\n}\npath.reverse();\n\n// Build pixel grid with solution overlay\nlet grid_w = 2 * w + 1;\nlet grid_h = 2 * h + 1;\n\n// 0 = wall, 1 = passage, 2 = solution path\nlet mut pixels = vec![0u8; grid_w * grid_h];\n\nfor cy in 0..h {\n    for cx in 0..w {\n        let walls = grid[cy * w + cx];\n        let px = 2 * cx + 1;\n        let py = 2 * cy + 1;\n\n        pixels[py * grid_w + px] = 1;\n\n        if walls & shared::N != 0 { pixels[(py - 1) * grid_w + px] = 1; }\n        if walls & shared::E != 0 { pixels[py * grid_w + (px + 1)] = 1; }\n        if walls & shared::S != 0 { pixels[(py + 1) * grid_w + px] = 1; }\n        if walls & shared::W != 0 { pixels[py * grid_w + (px - 1)] = 1; }\n    }\n}\n\n// Mark solution path cells and passages\nfor window in path.windows(2) {\n    let (ax, ay) = (window[0] % w, window[0] / w);\n    let (bx, by) = (window[1] % w, window[1] / w);\n\n    pixels[(2 * ay + 1) * grid_w + (2 * ax + 1)] = 2;\n    pixels[(2 * by + 1) * grid_w + (2 * bx + 1)] = 2;\n\n    // Mark passage between adjacent cells\n    let mid_x = ax + bx + 1;\n    let mid_y = ay + by + 1;\n    pixels[mid_y * grid_w + mid_x] = 2;\n}\n\nconst SCALE: u32 = 16;\nlet entrance = (1usize, 1usize);\nlet exit = (2 * (w - 1) + 1, 2 * (h - 1) + 1);\n\nlet canvas = Canvas::from_fn(grid_w as u32 * SCALE, grid_h as u32 * SCALE, |x, y| {\n    let lx = (x / SCALE) as usize;\n    let ly = (y / SCALE) as usize;\n\n    if (lx, ly) == entrance { return (80, 220, 120); }\n    if (lx, ly) == exit { return (220, 80, 80); }\n\n    match pixels[ly * grid_w + lx] {\n        2 => (0, 220, 180),   // solution path: cyan\n        1 => (200, 200, 220), // passage: light\n        _ => (40, 40, 60),    // wall: dark\n    }\n});\n\nCellOutput::from(canvas)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null,
      "collapsed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_4",
      "order": 4,
      "label": "Algorithm Details",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The Algorithm in Depth\n\n### Why Recursive Backtracker?\n\nThe recursive backtracker creates mazes with **long, winding corridors** because it carves as deep as possible before backtracking. This produces:\n- Few short dead ends\n- Long paths that feel satisfying to navigate\n- A \"river-like\" structure\n\n### Other Maze Algorithms\n\n| Algorithm | Character | Complexity |\n|-----------|-----------|------------|\n| **Recursive Backtracker** | Long corridors, few dead ends | O(n) time, O(n) stack |\n| **Kruskal's** | Many short dead ends, uniform feel | O(n log n) |\n| **Prim's** | Branching, tree-like structure | O(n log n) |\n| **Aldous-Broder** | Perfectly uniform | O(n\u00b2) expected |\n| **Wilson's** | Perfectly uniform | O(n\u00b2) worst case |\n\n### The BFS Solver\n\nBreadth-first search finds the **shortest path** in an unweighted graph. It explores all cells at distance 1 before distance 2, ensuring the first path found to the goal is optimal.\n\nFor weighted mazes (where passages have different costs), you'd use **Dijkstra's** or **A\\*** instead.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "Generate and solve random mazes using recursive backtracking.",
